[38;2;124;124;124mno, $C_0(X) = c_0(X)$, the functions vanishing at infinity.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIs $c_0(X)$ separable when $X$ is uncountable discrete? No. If $X$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124muncountable, $c_0(X)$ is not separable. Because the set of characteristic [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunctions $\{\chi_{\{x\}} : x \in X\}$ are at distance 1 from each other, and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthere are uncountably many of them, so no countable dense set can exist.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, the point evaluations $\delta_x: c_0(X) \to \mathbb{C}$, $f \mapsto f(x)$, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare one-dimensional representations (characters) for each $x \in X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $A = c_0(X)$ with $X$ uncountable discrete is a non-separable $C^*$-algebra [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat has one-dimensional (hence finite-dimensional) representations.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, the answer is NO. We cannot deduce that all representations of a non-[0m[38;2;124;124;124mseparable $C^*$-algebra are infinite-dimensional.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me make this even more concrete and simple. Take $X$ to be any uncountable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mset with discrete topology. Then $A = c_0(X)$ is a non-separable $C^*$-algebra. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mFor each $x_0 \in X$, the evaluation map $\pi_{x_0}(f) = f(x_0)$ is a one-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional representation.[0m

